        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>What is the difference between the NordicTrack 1250 and 1750?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              The confirmed differences are the screen, the motor warranty and the price. The 1250 has a 10 in. HD touchscreen that tilts, the 1750 a 16 in. HD touchscreen that pivots. NordicTrack gives the 1250 a 2-year motor warranty and lists lifetime motor cover for the 1750. Speed, incline and decline ranges are identical. Motor ratings and deck widths conflict across sources.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is the NordicTrack Commercial 1750 worth it over the 1250?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              It is worth it if you will use the pivoting 16 in. screen for iFIT floor classes, or if lifetime motor cover matters because you run a lot, share the machine or plan to keep it for many years. If you mainly walk and run on the belt, the 1250 does the same job for typically a few hundred dollars less.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What is the weight limit on the NordicTrack Commercial 1250?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              NordicTrack's spec sheet for the 1250 lists a maximum user weight of 400 lb and states it is not designed for users over 400 pounds. The figure for the 1750 is less clear: one US retailer lists 300 lb and a European listing about 400 lb, so ask NordicTrack in writing if you are near either figure.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What motor does the NordicTrack Commercial 1250 have?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              NordicTrack's own product page does not state it. Some sources list 3.0 continuous horsepower for earlier model years and 3.6 for a 2025 update, which suggests the rating has changed between versions. Ask NordicTrack or the retailer for the continuous horsepower of the exact model number you are buying, in writing, rather than relying on a listing.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Do the NordicTrack 1250 and 1750 have the same incline?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Yes. Both run from -3% decline to 12% incline and from 0 to 12 mph, according to NordicTrack. That means an iFIT route that changes grade will drive both machines through the same range, and the training experience on the belt is the same. If you need steeper than 12%, look at a dedicated incline machine instead.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Do you need an iFIT membership for the NordicTrack 1250 or 1750?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Both are iFIT-compatible, and the full iFIT content needs a paid membership on either machine. Check the current price rather than relying on an old figure. Before buying, ask what the console does without an active membership for your model year. If you will never subscribe, the main reason to pay extra for the 1750's pivoting screen largely disappears.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

